00:03:03.461 --> 00:03:03.960
Podcast.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:04.580
Hey, Aja.

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:05.330
How are you doing?

00:03:05.330 --> 00:03:05.840
AJA HAMMERLY: Hey, guys.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:06.850
I'm doing great.

00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:09.120
I'm really excited to be
on the podcast this week.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:09.330
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:10.800
We're very excited you're here.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:13.140
Why don't you tell
us a bit about you.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:14.660
We know you a
little bit already,

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:16.620
but our listeners don't.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:17.314
Who are you?

00:03:17.314 --> 00:03:18.730
AJA HAMMERLY: So
I'm Aja Hammerly.

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:21.870
I'm a developer advocate
on the Google Cloud team.

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:27.380
And I focus primarily on
monitoring, logging, trace,

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:29.910
debug-- all the things that
help you out when stuff

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:31.310
goes wrong on your website.

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:34.340
But before I came to Google I
had a large variety of jobs.

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:36.050
I was a developer,
I was a tester,

00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:38.390
I was a release
manager at companies

00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:41.310
that varied in size from a
really early stage startup

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:44.850
that I stayed with until
they got profitable or close

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:45.600
to profitable.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:48.590
And I also worked at big
companies and little companies

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:49.650
as a contractor.

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:51.720
And so that's kind of
why I come and work

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:55.007
on the stuff dealing with
when things go wrong,

00:03:55.007 --> 00:03:56.090
because I have been there.

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:57.020
I've held that pager.

00:03:57.020 --> 00:03:59.730
I've had that midnight phone
call saying, wait, wait,

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:00.767
the site's down.

00:04:00.767 --> 00:04:02.600
And I'm really passionate
about finding ways

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:04.699
that other people don't
get that phone call.

00:04:04.699 --> 00:04:06.490
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's
such a good person.

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:08.522
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:04:08.522 --> 00:04:10.480
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That
doesn't really sound fun.

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:12.130
So why don't you
tell us-- you say

00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:14.570
that you are one of the
people on the team that

00:04:14.570 --> 00:04:17.200
specializes in monitoring
and things like this.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:18.821
What is monitoring?

00:04:18.821 --> 00:04:21.029
AJA HAMMERLY: So I'm sure
that many of your listeners

00:04:21.029 --> 00:04:22.820
have been there where
they've got a website

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:25.190
or they've got some
sort of cloud-based

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:28.156
or even locally-based
application that's spreading.

00:04:28.156 --> 00:04:30.030
And they need to know
when bad things happen.

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:32.570
Because we all know that
computers are not actually

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:34.010
perfect all the time.

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:36.100
Disks go bad, disks fill up.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:38.470
We as software
developers write bugs.

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:40.990
I have certainly
written my fair share.

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:42.950
And we need to know
when that stuff is

00:04:42.950 --> 00:04:45.744
happening because it has the
ability to take down our site.

00:04:45.744 --> 00:04:47.660
And pretty much anyone
doesn't want their site

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:50.870
to go down without them at
least knowing ahead of time

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:53.170
and preferably them
doing it on purpose.

00:04:53.170 --> 00:04:55.740
Because sites that
go down our workloads

00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:58.126
that stop running
usually mean lost money.

00:04:58.126 --> 00:04:59.500
And monitoring is
the way that we

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:02.170
keep track of how the resources,
how the application is

00:05:02.170 --> 00:05:05.010
behaving, so we can have
a high-level picture

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:06.091
that everything is OK.

00:05:06.091 --> 00:05:07.590
A lot of the places
I've worked have

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:09.300
had some sort of
monitoring dashboard

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:12.700
that's mounted up on a screen
that's visible to everyone,

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:15.200
so that everyone can kind of
have a high-level picture that,

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:17.671
yes, everything is OK, and
that things are going well.

00:05:17.671 --> 00:05:18.670
Or no, everything isn't.

00:05:18.670 --> 00:05:20.086
You can see the
trends and you can

00:05:20.086 --> 00:05:23.494
deal with it before something
actually takes the site down.

00:05:23.494 --> 00:05:25.160
And monitoring is
also the kind of thing

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:26.440
that, if you're one
of those people who

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:28.480
used to carry a pager or
still carries a pager,

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:30.510
or your team passes
around an on-call phone

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:33.210
or maybe you've upgraded
to the current decade

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:36.070
and you have a system that
changes who's on call and just

00:05:36.070 --> 00:05:39.700
calls your personal phone,
those kinds of alerts

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:41.530
are also triggered
by monitoring.

00:05:41.530 --> 00:05:45.490
Hopefully those alerts happen
when something is bad enough

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:47.110
that it needs urgent attention.

00:05:47.110 --> 00:05:49.780
But hopefully not bad enough
that the site is already down.

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:51.340
And I have been there.

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:52.630
And I have carried that pager.

00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:56.250
And I actually still have kind
of a negative visceral reaction

00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:59.330
to a specific ring tone for
a specific brand of phone,

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:02.960
because that was the sound
that our on-call phone made.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:04.860
And it always meant that
whatever I was doing

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:06.276
was going to have
to be put aside.

00:06:06.276 --> 00:06:08.252
So like, if I was out
to dinner with people,

00:06:08.252 --> 00:06:10.460
I was going to have to sit
there, pull out the laptop

00:06:10.460 --> 00:06:13.200
and, you know, reboot servers
from the dinner table.

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:14.295
And I've done it.

00:06:14.295 --> 00:06:15.370
MARK MANDEL: Wow.

00:06:15.370 --> 00:06:16.245
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yep.

00:06:16.245 --> 00:06:16.750
Yep.

00:06:16.750 --> 00:06:18.666
MARK MANDEL: I have
similar visceral reactions

00:06:18.666 --> 00:06:19.494
to certain noises.

00:06:19.494 --> 00:06:20.410
So that's really cool.

00:06:20.410 --> 00:06:23.740
I think it's worth making
the point that monitoring,

00:06:23.740 --> 00:06:26.900
it's both preventive as
well as incident-based.

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:28.480
Would that be a fair summation?

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:29.271
AJA HAMMERLY: Yeah.

00:06:29.271 --> 00:06:30.572
It is both.

00:06:30.572 --> 00:06:32.030
In my world, I
would really like it

00:06:32.030 --> 00:06:34.190
to be more preventative
than incident-based

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:36.200
but computers are computers.

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:39.170
And I've been on the
on-call team or the ops team

00:06:39.170 --> 00:06:40.750
through two data
center fires now.

00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:43.994
So sometimes you just
can't be preventative.

00:06:43.994 --> 00:06:45.410
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Out of curiosity,

00:06:45.410 --> 00:06:48.720
when you say a data center
fire, is it a literal fire?

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:49.470
AJA HAMMERLY: Yes.

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:51.010
Both of them.

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:53.570
One company that I worked
at, our data center,

00:06:53.570 --> 00:06:56.017
the auxiliary power
units caught fire.

00:06:56.017 --> 00:06:57.350
The whole data center went down.

00:06:57.350 --> 00:06:59.250
And they actually
emailed-- when they finally

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:01.791
got some of the stuff back up
they emailed all the people who

00:07:01.791 --> 00:07:05.310
were hosted there a picture
of the charred auxiliary power

00:07:05.310 --> 00:07:08.310
unit that had taken down a
good hunk of the data center.

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:09.770
And then the other
data center fire

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:12.310
we dealt with there was
actually at one of our vendors.

00:07:12.310 --> 00:07:14.590
And it was over a
holiday weekend.

00:07:14.590 --> 00:07:17.170
And so the fire department
got the fire out,

00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:19.440
but they couldn't actually
go back in and certify

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:21.148
that they could turn
the electricity back

00:07:21.148 --> 00:07:23.750
on to the data center until
after the holiday weekend.

00:07:23.750 --> 00:07:25.770
So one of our vendors
was entirely down

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:28.390
for a four-day weekend
or three-day weekend.

00:07:28.390 --> 00:07:29.330
MARK MANDEL: Fun.

00:07:29.330 --> 00:07:29.570
AJA HAMMERLY: Yeah.

00:07:29.570 --> 00:07:31.790
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
monitoring will detect that.

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:35.658
I'm assuming it will.

00:07:35.658 --> 00:07:36.494
[LAUGHING]

00:07:36.494 --> 00:07:37.285
AJA HAMMERLY: Yeah.

00:07:37.285 --> 00:07:38.831
It detects if
resources go completely

00:07:38.831 --> 00:07:39.830
away pretty effectively.

00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:40.340
Yes.

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:41.256
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:07:41.256 --> 00:07:44.830
But what is the kind of
things that you monitor?

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:47.210
I understand that you monitor
the [INAUDIBLE] machine

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:50.379
and maybe you monitor if
a network is up or not.

00:07:50.379 --> 00:07:51.170
AJA HAMMERLY: Yeah.

00:07:51.170 --> 00:07:53.370
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Do you
monitor other things?

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:56.240
AJA HAMMERLY: So
I think about it--

00:07:56.240 --> 00:07:59.272
if you've been around
for a while, are

00:07:59.272 --> 00:08:00.480
you familiar with networking?

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:02.021
They talk about
layers of networking.

00:08:02.021 --> 00:08:05.040
And I actually think about
things as layers of monitoring.

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:07.510
So at the base layer,
I monitor to make sure

00:08:07.510 --> 00:08:10.400
that the resources that I
expect to be up in terms of VMs

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:12.580
or physical machines are up.

00:08:12.580 --> 00:08:15.050
They are on, or
they are responding

00:08:15.050 --> 00:08:16.980
to just low-level ping.

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:20.240
I also monitor things like
CPU usage, memory usage,

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:21.360
and disk usage.

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:23.050
Disk usage is one
that has burned me

00:08:23.050 --> 00:08:26.336
enough times that I always
keep a really good eye on it.

00:08:26.336 --> 00:08:28.210
Because if you end up
with a bug that ends up

00:08:28.210 --> 00:08:29.972
spewing a bunch of
stuff to your logs,

00:08:29.972 --> 00:08:31.430
you can actually
fill up your disk.

00:08:31.430 --> 00:08:33.439
And the applications
I generally work

00:08:33.439 --> 00:08:35.980
with have been Rails and Node,
and neither of those deals too

00:08:35.980 --> 00:08:40.090
well with not being able
to write logs at all.

00:08:40.090 --> 00:08:42.840
So I monitor CPU disk
usage memory usage.

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:44.590
And I'm mostly
monitoring those things

00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:46.540
to make sure that they
stay about the same.

00:08:46.540 --> 00:08:48.110
Because if they
start doing something

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:50.539
that looks outside of what
looks normal for them,

00:08:50.539 --> 00:08:52.580
it's usually an indication
that I have a software

00:08:52.580 --> 00:08:53.870
bug that I haven't detected.

00:08:53.870 --> 00:08:58.100
And then after the basic level
of monitoring system resources,

00:08:58.100 --> 00:09:00.170
sometimes I'll monitor
load balancers and network

00:09:00.170 --> 00:09:00.970
connections as well.

00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:03.050
I then start monitoring stuff
in the application layer.

00:09:03.050 --> 00:09:04.150
And that's going
to really depend

00:09:04.150 --> 00:09:05.316
on what your application is.

00:09:05.316 --> 00:09:07.420
Because if you've got
an app that's primarily

00:09:07.420 --> 00:09:09.440
doing video
transcoding, the things

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:12.140
that you're interested in are
very different than the things

00:09:12.140 --> 00:09:14.244
that someone who's got a
web serving workload is.

00:09:14.244 --> 00:09:15.660
A web serving
workload, they might

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:19.387
be interested in the number
of 400 and 500 range errors

00:09:19.387 --> 00:09:19.970
that they get.

00:09:19.970 --> 00:09:22.060
Or they might be
interested in latency.

00:09:22.060 --> 00:09:25.920
Or they might be interested that
a certain endpoint is getting

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:27.720
most of the traffic,
or a certain endpoint

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:29.370
is getting a normal
amount of traffic.

00:09:29.370 --> 00:09:32.060
Because they want
to know if there's

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:35.240
a problem in their
application logic or pipeline.

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:37.200
And so it really
depends on the workload,

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:39.160
what I end up monitoring.

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:41.300
But I do try to
monitor out all layers.

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:42.050
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:09:42.050 --> 00:09:44.330
So let's talk about
what monitoring

00:09:44.330 --> 00:09:46.920
is available on
Google Cloud Platform.

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:49.164
We have this stuff
called Stackdriver.

00:09:49.164 --> 00:09:51.080
I believe that used to
be a different company?

00:09:51.080 --> 00:09:51.380
AJA HAMMERLY: Yeah.

00:09:51.380 --> 00:09:53.260
Stackdriver was
acquired by Google.

00:09:53.260 --> 00:09:57.740
And they've been working
to integrate their products

00:09:57.740 --> 00:10:00.620
seamlessly into the existing
Google Cloud Platform Console.

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:02.507
And the integration is
lovely at this point.

00:10:02.507 --> 00:10:04.090
I've really enjoyed--
as someone who's

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:05.756
used a lot of different
monitoring tools

00:10:05.756 --> 00:10:09.240
over the years, how the
place where I create my VMs

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:11.840
is the same place that
I can see my alerts.

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:12.910
I can see a dashboard.

00:10:12.910 --> 00:10:14.701
It's the same place
that I can see my logs.

00:10:14.701 --> 00:10:17.990
It's the same place that I can
see error reports distilled.

00:10:17.990 --> 00:10:19.440
It's all one login.

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:22.700
And when I go talk to
folks about their Ops stack

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:25.126
or their monitoring
stack, most folks

00:10:25.126 --> 00:10:26.750
seem to have four or
five products now.

00:10:26.750 --> 00:10:28.410
One of the things I
love about Stackdriver

00:10:28.410 --> 00:10:29.570
is that it's one
product that does

00:10:29.570 --> 00:10:30.990
all the things with one login.

00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:33.410
So figuring out who
needs permissions

00:10:33.410 --> 00:10:35.360
for what is actually
really straightforward.

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:36.943
When you get a new
employee, you don't

00:10:36.943 --> 00:10:39.890
have to give them an account
to five different services.

00:10:39.890 --> 00:10:42.190
And specifically on
the monitoring side,

00:10:42.190 --> 00:10:45.805
Stackdriver monitors all of
our Google Cloud Platform

00:10:45.805 --> 00:10:46.305
Resources.

00:10:46.305 --> 00:10:49.390
It can monitor App Engine, it
can monitor Compute Engine.

00:10:49.390 --> 00:10:52.510
It monitors Load Balancers.

00:10:52.510 --> 00:10:55.750
And they can also
monitor resources

00:10:55.750 --> 00:10:57.450
that are in AWS as well.

00:10:57.450 --> 00:10:58.400
It's cross-cloud.

00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:00.390
There's been a lot of
push in the industry

00:11:00.390 --> 00:11:02.490
and also some
internally at Google

00:11:02.490 --> 00:11:04.540
that many of our products
now work across cloud.

00:11:04.540 --> 00:11:06.331
Another good example
of that is Kubernetes,

00:11:06.331 --> 00:11:07.760
which works cross-cloud.

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:09.924
And Kubernetes is not
technically Google's product.

00:11:09.924 --> 00:11:11.090
It's an open source product.

00:11:11.090 --> 00:11:13.470
But it's another example of
how Google is really pushing

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:14.710
for the cross-cloud support.

00:11:14.710 --> 00:11:15.460
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:11:15.460 --> 00:11:18.980
So we could probably go
through all of the Stackdriver

00:11:18.980 --> 00:11:20.480
features for hours
and hours on end,

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:22.610
but since we only have
about 20 or 30 minutes,

00:11:22.610 --> 00:11:24.840
do you want to give us some
of your favorite features

00:11:24.840 --> 00:11:26.219
of the Stackdriver monitoring?

00:11:26.219 --> 00:11:27.010
AJA HAMMERLY: Yeah.

00:11:27.010 --> 00:11:28.810
So I'm going to kind
of go-- depending

00:11:28.810 --> 00:11:30.040
on what resources
you're monitoring,

00:11:30.040 --> 00:11:31.320
I have different
favorite features.

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:33.890
So one of my favorite features
is for App Engine actually,

00:11:33.890 --> 00:11:35.859
and that latency
monitoring is built in.

00:11:35.859 --> 00:11:37.650
I can say, I would like
to monitor latency.

00:11:37.650 --> 00:11:39.149
And I would like
to get alerted when

00:11:39.149 --> 00:11:43.360
latency is over this threshold
or increases by this much.

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:44.740
And the other
thing I really like

00:11:44.740 --> 00:11:47.310
about the latency monitoring
for App Engine that's built in

00:11:47.310 --> 00:11:50.970
is that you can monitor at 95%.

00:11:50.970 --> 00:11:53.450
So you can say, I want
95% of my requests

00:11:53.450 --> 00:11:55.090
to be at least this fast.

00:11:55.090 --> 00:11:57.440
You can monitor at the 5%.

00:11:57.440 --> 00:12:01.590
You can say, so if 5% of my
requests are slower than this,

00:12:01.590 --> 00:12:02.400
that's an alert.

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:03.810
Or you can look at the average.

00:12:03.810 --> 00:12:05.800
And several of the apps
that I've worked on

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:08.786
have had an endpoint
that is like really

00:12:08.786 --> 00:12:10.160
fast most of the
time-- under 100

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:11.890
milliseconds most of the time.

00:12:11.890 --> 00:12:13.746
But occasionally
there's that edge case

00:12:13.746 --> 00:12:15.870
through the code that's
just going to take forever.

00:12:15.870 --> 00:12:17.495
And you know it's
going to take forever

00:12:17.495 --> 00:12:19.380
but it really drags
down your averages.

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:22.470
So the fact that I can
make those outliers go away

00:12:22.470 --> 00:12:24.310
on my monitoring is
wonderful, because that

00:12:24.310 --> 00:12:26.830
means that I don't get
that page if we happen

00:12:26.830 --> 00:12:28.600
to get some anomalous
traffic and get

00:12:28.600 --> 00:12:31.595
a couple of extra things to
the bad path a couple times.

00:12:31.595 --> 00:12:33.720
And sometimes, you know,
you want to fix that path.

00:12:33.720 --> 00:12:34.960
But sometimes you look at
it and you're like, yeah

00:12:34.960 --> 00:12:37.050
that happens 1,000 times a day.

00:12:37.050 --> 00:12:37.750
I don't care.

00:12:37.750 --> 00:12:39.680
It's not worth the
dev effort to fix it.

00:12:39.680 --> 00:12:41.221
So that's one of my
favorite features

00:12:41.221 --> 00:12:44.310
in App Engine is monitoring,
or Stackdriver for App Engine

00:12:44.310 --> 00:12:46.507
is the latency monitoring.

00:12:46.507 --> 00:12:48.340
And then you can not
go into Compute Engine.

00:12:48.340 --> 00:12:50.410
And one of the things I
like about Stackdriver

00:12:50.410 --> 00:12:51.110
with computer engine.

00:12:51.110 --> 00:12:53.360
And I actually didn't know
this until I was setting up

00:12:53.360 --> 00:12:56.310
a demo recently is that
there's built in monitoring

00:12:56.310 --> 00:12:59.510
for a bunch of different common
tools like MySQL or Cassandra

00:12:59.510 --> 00:13:02.340
or RabbitMQ.

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:06.675
And sometimes the story
is a little bit weird.

00:13:06.675 --> 00:13:09.080
I recommend folks
go look at the docs.

00:13:09.080 --> 00:13:12.469
We will actually detect that
you're running MySQL on a given

00:13:12.469 --> 00:13:13.510
computer engine instance.

00:13:13.510 --> 00:13:14.960
And we do that by looking
at this instance name,

00:13:14.960 --> 00:13:17.040
looking at the processes
that are running,

00:13:17.040 --> 00:13:19.080
and also looking at
what ports are open.

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:21.010
And we can turn on the
monitoring for MySQL

00:13:21.010 --> 00:13:22.099
automatically.

00:13:22.099 --> 00:13:24.140
So we'll say, oh, you're
running a MySQL instance

00:13:24.140 --> 00:13:25.140
on this computer engine.

00:13:25.140 --> 00:13:26.960
And the MySQL metrics
will magically

00:13:26.960 --> 00:13:30.296
be available in your
Stackdriver dashboard.

00:13:30.296 --> 00:13:32.420
And I hate to use magic
because it's all computers.

00:13:32.420 --> 00:13:35.070
It's all done with
well-written code.

00:13:35.070 --> 00:13:37.570
But you know, any sufficiently
advanced technology

00:13:37.570 --> 00:13:38.980
is indistinguishable from magic.

00:13:38.980 --> 00:13:42.230
It was a lovely surprise when I
was setting up a MySQL Cluster.

00:13:42.230 --> 00:13:44.420
And even if it isn't
automatically detected,

00:13:44.420 --> 00:13:45.850
turning it on is super easy.

00:13:45.850 --> 00:13:48.700
You add a config file and
you restart the Stackdriver

00:13:48.700 --> 00:13:49.740
monitoring process.

00:13:49.740 --> 00:13:52.910
And now you have access
to metrics such as inserts

00:13:52.910 --> 00:13:55.060
and updates for MySQL.

00:13:55.060 --> 00:13:59.446
For Engine X you have access
to the HTTP connections,

00:13:59.446 --> 00:14:00.320
how many are waiting.

00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:02.000
That kind of thing.

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:04.580
The metrics that make sense are
available when you need them,

00:14:04.580 --> 00:14:06.890
but they're not crowding
your dashboard out

00:14:06.890 --> 00:14:08.390
when they don't
actually make sense.

00:14:08.390 --> 00:14:10.700
And so that intelligent
decision about which

00:14:10.700 --> 00:14:12.977
metrics to make available
is really, really nice.

00:14:12.977 --> 00:14:14.310
MARK MANDEL: That's really cool.

00:14:14.310 --> 00:14:15.101
AJA HAMMERLY: Yeah.

00:14:15.101 --> 00:14:17.520
The other thing that
I like is that there's

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:21.890
monitoring in for Google
Container Engine as well.

00:14:21.890 --> 00:14:24.110
So if you're running a
Container Engine cluster,

00:14:24.110 --> 00:14:26.442
there's a dashboard
that you get just by--

00:14:26.442 --> 00:14:27.650
and this is in the free tier.

00:14:27.650 --> 00:14:30.030
You can just go to
Stackdriver and you

00:14:30.030 --> 00:14:32.950
can see monitoring of your
Container Engine resources,

00:14:32.950 --> 00:14:35.320
both by containers and
also by the underlying VMs.

00:14:35.320 --> 00:14:36.112
And that's awesome.

00:14:36.112 --> 00:14:38.445
MARK MANDEL: So it sounds
like there is a bunch of stuff

00:14:38.445 --> 00:14:40.410
that's like out of
the box, ready to go.

00:14:40.410 --> 00:14:40.640
AJA HAMMERLY: Yeah.

00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:42.931
MARK MANDEL: As long as you're
using some cloud product

00:14:42.931 --> 00:14:44.440
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:46.554
We're just like,
yeah, here you go.

00:14:46.554 --> 00:14:48.720
Is there anywhere where you
have to do manual steps?

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:50.800
I think on GCE you have
to install an agent?

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:51.419
Is that right?

00:14:51.419 --> 00:14:53.460
AJA HAMMERLY: Depending
on how you started it up,

00:14:53.460 --> 00:14:54.970
you may have to
install the agent.

00:14:54.970 --> 00:14:57.140
You do have to install
the agent in AWS

00:14:57.140 --> 00:14:58.510
if you're going to use it there.

00:14:58.510 --> 00:15:00.349
When I did it for
a recent demo, it

00:15:00.349 --> 00:15:01.640
took me less than five minutes.

00:15:01.640 --> 00:15:03.381
The instructions
are super clear.

00:15:03.381 --> 00:15:05.630
And if you're one of the
people like most of the folks

00:15:05.630 --> 00:15:07.463
I hang out with in the
Dev-Ops community who

00:15:07.463 --> 00:15:08.960
uses something like
Ansible or Chef

00:15:08.960 --> 00:15:11.610
or Puppet to provision
your VMs, you

00:15:11.610 --> 00:15:14.500
can actually install the agent
in a very straightforward way

00:15:14.500 --> 00:15:15.380
with those tools.

00:15:15.380 --> 00:15:17.480
There's a cookbook for
it, for Chef for example.

00:15:17.480 --> 00:15:18.230
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:15:18.230 --> 00:15:19.605
AJA HAMMERLY: And
so if you don't

00:15:19.605 --> 00:15:23.200
want to do it by hand, doing it
automatically is really easy.

00:15:23.200 --> 00:15:25.640
And it's just another
monitoring agent.

00:15:25.640 --> 00:15:27.802
I've used many, many
monitoring services,

00:15:27.802 --> 00:15:29.760
and they all pretty much
have this requirement.

00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:30.801
But it doesn't take long.

00:15:30.801 --> 00:15:32.674
You curl down some code.

00:15:32.674 --> 00:15:34.840
You put it in a directory
and change the permissions

00:15:34.840 --> 00:15:35.620
and start it up.

00:15:35.620 --> 00:15:38.070
It's really
straightforward if you're

00:15:38.070 --> 00:15:40.264
in a world where you do
that kind of thing often.

00:15:40.264 --> 00:15:41.180
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:15:41.180 --> 00:15:44.450
So it seems like having
the basic monitoring

00:15:44.450 --> 00:15:47.400
of your application, regardless
of the stack that you're using

00:15:47.400 --> 00:15:48.600
is pretty easy.

00:15:48.600 --> 00:15:51.752
What if you want to have some
specific metric that only

00:15:51.752 --> 00:15:52.960
your application knows about?

00:15:52.960 --> 00:15:55.699
How does Stackdriver
handle those cases?

00:15:55.699 --> 00:15:57.490
AJA HAMMERLY: So you
can do custom metrics.

00:15:57.490 --> 00:15:59.489
And that's one of the
things that I really like.

00:15:59.489 --> 00:16:02.050
Because when I've
done apps before,

00:16:02.050 --> 00:16:03.370
we frequently would sit down.

00:16:03.370 --> 00:16:05.560
We're like, we'll how do
we know if everything's OK?

00:16:05.560 --> 00:16:08.100
And we would sit
down over a table

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:11.670
and come up with a decision
that these five end

00:16:11.670 --> 00:16:15.170
points need to respond in
less than 100 milliseconds 95%

00:16:15.170 --> 00:16:16.660
of the time.

00:16:16.660 --> 00:16:19.660
And then we would then-- with
Stackdriver you could then

00:16:19.660 --> 00:16:20.900
build up a metric for that.

00:16:20.900 --> 00:16:22.358
Or I worked on
another app where we

00:16:22.358 --> 00:16:26.190
did a bunch of image
resizing as part of the load.

00:16:26.190 --> 00:16:30.434
And so we needed to know
how well the memories

00:16:30.434 --> 00:16:31.350
it was on the machine.

00:16:31.350 --> 00:16:33.110
And we had a bar where
if the memories, which

00:16:33.110 --> 00:16:34.320
went beyond a
certain amount, that

00:16:34.320 --> 00:16:36.880
was concerning because we had
messed up the image resizing

00:16:36.880 --> 00:16:38.110
process.

00:16:38.110 --> 00:16:40.390
And with Stackdriver you
can create custom metrics.

00:16:40.390 --> 00:16:42.723
And you can also create custom
metrics that you actually

00:16:42.723 --> 00:16:44.030
report from your application.

00:16:44.030 --> 00:16:45.446
There's an API
that you can report

00:16:45.446 --> 00:16:49.990
in the Stackdriver, a metric
that is interesting to you,

00:16:49.990 --> 00:16:51.900
and have that show
up in your dashboard

00:16:51.900 --> 00:16:54.970
and get alerts based on
that, which is awesome.

00:16:54.970 --> 00:16:56.806
And all those are going
to be app-specific.

00:16:56.806 --> 00:16:59.180
That's one of the things that's
really hard about talking

00:16:59.180 --> 00:17:01.420
about the cloud is there's so
much that you can do with it.

00:17:01.420 --> 00:17:03.545
And everyone's workload is
just slightly different.

00:17:03.545 --> 00:17:05.515
I know a lot about
web serving workloads.

00:17:05.515 --> 00:17:06.890
But there are so
many cool things

00:17:06.890 --> 00:17:08.806
that people are doing
that aren't specifically

00:17:08.806 --> 00:17:09.990
web serving.

00:17:09.990 --> 00:17:11.329
Data processing, for example.

00:17:11.329 --> 00:17:13.970
There might be other metrics
that you need to report in on.

00:17:13.970 --> 00:17:16.386
And that's something that you
could do with custom metrics

00:17:16.386 --> 00:17:17.534
as well.

00:17:17.534 --> 00:17:18.450
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:17:18.450 --> 00:17:19.359
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:17:19.359 --> 00:17:23.470
So right now if people want
to get a using of Stackdriver,

00:17:23.470 --> 00:17:26.262
it's in beta so
it's currently free.

00:17:26.262 --> 00:17:27.720
But there was a
recent announcement

00:17:27.720 --> 00:17:29.520
for what the pricing
structure is.

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:30.840
Do you want to talk a
little bit about that?

00:17:30.840 --> 00:17:32.580
It seems pretty
straightforward though.

00:17:32.580 --> 00:17:33.371
AJA HAMMERLY: Yeah.

00:17:33.371 --> 00:17:35.750
So the basic pricing
structure, when it goes out

00:17:35.750 --> 00:17:37.430
of beta and into
general availability

00:17:37.430 --> 00:17:39.069
is there's going
to be a free tier.

00:17:39.069 --> 00:17:41.110
And that's going to provide
you access to metrics

00:17:41.110 --> 00:17:42.220
traces and error reports.

00:17:42.220 --> 00:17:43.280
Because we're
talking specifically

00:17:43.280 --> 00:17:44.130
today about monitoring.

00:17:44.130 --> 00:17:46.046
But Stackdriver is a
much bigger product than.

00:17:46.046 --> 00:17:48.795
That there's a lot of
other features involved.

00:17:48.795 --> 00:17:51.170
And those are all going be
available in your Google Cloud

00:17:51.170 --> 00:17:52.820
Platform dashboard.

00:17:52.820 --> 00:17:56.090
And you can get that free
monitoring on Google Cloud

00:17:56.090 --> 00:17:57.787
Platform resources.

00:17:57.787 --> 00:18:00.370
So if you're using App Engine,
if you're using Compute Engine,

00:18:00.370 --> 00:18:03.350
you'll be able to see
your metrics in real-time

00:18:03.350 --> 00:18:05.050
on the dashboard for free.

00:18:05.050 --> 00:18:06.875
Now we're going
have a premium tier.

00:18:06.875 --> 00:18:09.300
That will give you your Amazon
Web Services integrations.

00:18:09.300 --> 00:18:10.397
There's your cross-cloud.

00:18:10.397 --> 00:18:12.730
It's also going to give you
support, if you need support

00:18:12.730 --> 00:18:14.465
on your monitoring logging.

00:18:14.465 --> 00:18:15.840
If you want to
get alerts, so you

00:18:15.840 --> 00:18:21.210
want to be notified in Slack or
HipChat or PagerDuty or by SMS,

00:18:21.210 --> 00:18:23.580
all that's going to be a
part of the premium tier.

00:18:23.580 --> 00:18:27.020
It also gives you custom metrics
and several other features.

00:18:27.020 --> 00:18:31.010
So the premium tier is priced
at $8 per monitored resource

00:18:31.010 --> 00:18:32.200
per month.

00:18:32.200 --> 00:18:33.740
And that's prorated hourly.

00:18:33.740 --> 00:18:35.840
And that's kind of the basics.

00:18:35.840 --> 00:18:37.417
So $8 a month per
monitored resource.

00:18:37.417 --> 00:18:39.750
Now the question everyone
asks is-- there's two of them.

00:18:39.750 --> 00:18:41.652
What the heck is a
monitored resource?

00:18:41.652 --> 00:18:43.360
And the second question
is, how does that

00:18:43.360 --> 00:18:44.526
work if you're auto-scaling?

00:18:44.526 --> 00:18:48.570
So monitor resources are
App Engine instances,

00:18:48.570 --> 00:18:53.050
compute engine instances,
cloud SQL, and load balancers.

00:18:53.050 --> 00:18:55.826
So basically everything
that can be mapped to a VM

00:18:55.826 --> 00:18:56.950
is going to be an instance.

00:18:56.950 --> 00:18:58.830
So when you scale up
your App Engine app

00:18:58.830 --> 00:19:00.740
you have more VMs
that are provisioned.

00:19:00.740 --> 00:19:02.519
And then you scale it back down.

00:19:02.519 --> 00:19:04.310
And then the auto
scaling is, they're going

00:19:04.310 --> 00:19:05.770
to take a snapshot every hour.

00:19:05.770 --> 00:19:07.640
And they're going to figure out
how many monitored resources

00:19:07.640 --> 00:19:08.850
you're using every hour.

00:19:08.850 --> 00:19:10.730
And they're going to
prorate based on that.

00:19:10.730 --> 00:19:12.950
So if you spin up a VM
and then spin it back down

00:19:12.950 --> 00:19:15.644
or you use a bunch of
different VMs over the month,

00:19:15.644 --> 00:19:17.310
we're going to try
to come up with a way

00:19:17.310 --> 00:19:19.230
to smash those
together, bin pack them,

00:19:19.230 --> 00:19:20.920
to give you the pricing.

00:19:20.920 --> 00:19:22.930
So you don't have to worry
about us charging you

00:19:22.930 --> 00:19:25.860
for every single different
instance that you spin up.

00:19:25.860 --> 00:19:29.600
So it should work in a logical
way for auto scaling groups.

00:19:29.600 --> 00:19:33.145
You should pay for a
fraction of that $8

00:19:33.145 --> 00:19:35.270
for the fraction of the
time that you're scaled up.

00:19:35.270 --> 00:19:36.061
MARK MANDEL: Sweet.

00:19:36.061 --> 00:19:38.870
So if I'm on App Engine standard
and I have a short spike where

00:19:38.870 --> 00:19:41.360
it goes up a fair bit, but
then comes back down relatively

00:19:41.360 --> 00:19:44.475
quickly, it sort of mushes that
all together into one big chunk

00:19:44.475 --> 00:19:46.225
that I don't have to
worry about too much.

00:19:46.225 --> 00:19:47.016
AJA HAMMERLY: Yeah.

00:19:47.016 --> 00:19:48.420
And it's prorated hourly.

00:19:48.420 --> 00:19:51.530
So divide $8 by the number of
hours in the month and that's

00:19:51.530 --> 00:19:52.320
your hourly rate.

00:19:52.320 --> 00:19:54.530
It's really not
that much at all.

00:19:54.530 --> 00:19:56.280
The other question
people ask is about how

00:19:56.280 --> 00:19:58.490
that works for containers.

00:19:58.490 --> 00:20:00.889
If you're running containers
on Compute Engine,

00:20:00.889 --> 00:20:02.430
and if you're using
Container Engine,

00:20:02.430 --> 00:20:03.670
you are actually
running a containers

00:20:03.670 --> 00:20:05.060
on computer engine resources.

00:20:05.060 --> 00:20:05.940
We're still just
going to charge you

00:20:05.940 --> 00:20:08.210
for the computer engine VMs and
the amount of time they're up.

00:20:08.210 --> 00:20:09.730
So if you scale
up your containers

00:20:09.730 --> 00:20:11.370
but you don't actually
scale up the number of hosts

00:20:11.370 --> 00:20:12.640
that you're using
to run them, you're

00:20:12.640 --> 00:20:14.013
still going to be
paying the same price.

00:20:14.013 --> 00:20:14.860
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:20:14.860 --> 00:20:16.110
MARK MANDEL: That's very cool.

00:20:16.110 --> 00:20:18.500
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So we're
kind of running out of time,

00:20:18.500 --> 00:20:21.730
but there's actually a question
that I would like to ask you.

00:20:21.730 --> 00:20:26.970
Which is, OK so I did not
know monitoring was a thing.

00:20:26.970 --> 00:20:29.430
And I have something
running in production.

00:20:29.430 --> 00:20:31.250
What do I do?

00:20:31.250 --> 00:20:33.855
What is the first thing I should
start doing, like right now?

00:20:33.855 --> 00:20:35.230
AJA HAMMERLY: So
here's the thing

00:20:35.230 --> 00:20:37.370
that I tell folks what
I'm doing mentoring,

00:20:37.370 --> 00:20:39.934
and I will say right now that
I am not an expert on dev ops.

00:20:39.934 --> 00:20:41.350
But the very first
thing you do is

00:20:41.350 --> 00:20:42.944
you should set up
an uptime monitor.

00:20:42.944 --> 00:20:44.360
And all that's
going to do is it's

00:20:44.360 --> 00:20:46.510
going to-- I usually
set this up to hit

00:20:46.510 --> 00:20:50.180
a specific URL frequently,
the base page of my app.

00:20:50.180 --> 00:20:54.440
And it tells me if that
page stops responding.

00:20:54.440 --> 00:20:56.110
So it basically tells
you if that page

00:20:56.110 --> 00:20:57.900
is throwing a 500 or 404.

00:20:57.900 --> 00:21:00.400
If I want to get a little more
sophisticated in Stackdriver,

00:21:00.400 --> 00:21:01.066
you can do this.

00:21:01.066 --> 00:21:03.760
You can have it check for
a specific string a texts.

00:21:03.760 --> 00:21:06.910
And I often do the company name
in the footer, because almost

00:21:06.910 --> 00:21:09.321
everyone has their company
name, and the copyright info

00:21:09.321 --> 00:21:10.820
or their name and
the copyright info

00:21:10.820 --> 00:21:11.970
in the footer of their website.

00:21:11.970 --> 00:21:13.511
And if that stop
showing up, you know

00:21:13.511 --> 00:21:16.290
that there's a problem with
basic rendering of your page.

00:21:16.290 --> 00:21:19.110
Pro tip, don't use the date
if your date auto increments

00:21:19.110 --> 00:21:21.990
on the new year, because then
you get a new year's day page

00:21:21.990 --> 00:21:24.520
when the date auto increments.

00:21:24.520 --> 00:21:26.250
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Happy new year.

00:21:26.250 --> 00:21:29.150
AJA HAMMERLY: But get your
up time monitor setup.

00:21:29.150 --> 00:21:31.490
And then the next thing
I think folks should do,

00:21:31.490 --> 00:21:35.230
is they should set up some sort
of metrics based on latency

00:21:35.230 --> 00:21:37.320
or application
errors on the things

00:21:37.320 --> 00:21:39.770
that they know cause a bad
time for their customers.

00:21:39.770 --> 00:21:42.700
And they should also set
up some basic metrics.

00:21:42.700 --> 00:21:44.930
With Stackdriver you can
do it based on trend.

00:21:44.930 --> 00:21:47.430
So if your memory
usage increases

00:21:47.430 --> 00:21:51.440
by 200% over 10 minutes,
that's the kind of alert

00:21:51.440 --> 00:21:52.957
I would set up on
the system level.

00:21:52.957 --> 00:21:55.040
Because that means you
don't have to actually know

00:21:55.040 --> 00:21:58.180
what your normal state is.

00:21:58.180 --> 00:22:00.850
But you can still get an alert
if something weird starts

00:22:00.850 --> 00:22:01.420
happening.

00:22:01.420 --> 00:22:03.090
Because the whole goal
of monitoring is to let

00:22:03.090 --> 00:22:04.790
you know that something
weird is happening,

00:22:04.790 --> 00:22:06.650
so that then you can use
your big human brain to try

00:22:06.650 --> 00:22:07.692
to figure out what it is.

00:22:07.692 --> 00:22:09.400
MARK MANDEL: So really
it's about change.

00:22:09.400 --> 00:22:11.256
It's not necessarily
what your base case is.

00:22:11.256 --> 00:22:12.047
AJA HAMMERLY: Yeah.

00:22:12.047 --> 00:22:14.002
That's the, I have no
idea about monitoring

00:22:14.002 --> 00:22:15.460
and I would like
to get in, I would

00:22:15.460 --> 00:22:17.585
like to do this thing
because it sounds good story.

00:22:17.585 --> 00:22:19.860
It's focus on change
and focus on making sure

00:22:19.860 --> 00:22:21.600
that the basics of
your site are up.

00:22:21.600 --> 00:22:23.225
Because the worst
thing that can happen

00:22:23.225 --> 00:22:25.650
is someone types in your URL
or clicks a link in search

00:22:25.650 --> 00:22:27.985
results and ends up
at a 400 or a 500.

00:22:27.985 --> 00:22:29.485
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
that makes sense.

00:22:29.485 --> 00:22:33.090
All right, Before we finish up,
is there anything in particular

00:22:33.090 --> 00:22:35.526
that you'd want to mention
