00:24:26.654 --> 00:24:28.570
and then they'll be like,
yes, there's a fire.

00:24:28.570 --> 00:24:30.236
FRANCESC CAMPOY: There
is indeed a fire.

00:24:30.236 --> 00:24:30.980
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:24:30.980 --> 00:24:32.515
An uptime check is not enough.

00:24:32.515 --> 00:24:32.980
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:24:32.980 --> 00:24:33.485
Yeah.

00:24:33.485 --> 00:24:35.360
Resetting the computer
will not fix the fire.

00:24:35.360 --> 00:24:35.540
MARK MANDEL: No.

00:24:35.540 --> 00:24:36.144
No.

00:24:36.144 --> 00:24:36.643
Absolutely.

00:24:36.643 --> 00:24:37.430
All right.

00:24:37.430 --> 00:24:39.850
So why don't we get on
to question of the week.

00:24:39.850 --> 00:24:41.694
I think you had an
interesting one there.

00:24:41.694 --> 00:24:42.610
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:24:42.610 --> 00:24:44.520
MARK MANDEL: Why don't
I set it up for you.

00:24:44.520 --> 00:24:44.990
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:24:44.990 --> 00:24:46.656
MARK MANDEL: You can
give me the answer.

00:24:46.656 --> 00:24:48.370
So we were talking
about if, say,

00:24:48.370 --> 00:24:50.110
you've got a Google
Cloud storage bucket

00:24:50.110 --> 00:24:51.780
and there's a bunch
of data in it.

00:24:51.780 --> 00:24:53.410
And you're like, OK, I
want to pull this data down

00:24:53.410 --> 00:24:54.350
so I can look at it.

00:24:54.350 --> 00:24:56.160
So you pull it
down using gsutil.

00:24:56.160 --> 00:24:57.017
Great, that's fine.

00:24:57.017 --> 00:24:58.600
And then you're like,
oh wait, there's

00:24:58.600 --> 00:25:00.120
some stuff I need
to change in it.

00:25:00.120 --> 00:25:02.250
So I make some small changes
to maybe three or four

00:25:02.250 --> 00:25:05.317
out of the 200 files
or something like that.

00:25:05.317 --> 00:25:07.400
But I want to sync that
up to what's in the cloud.

00:25:07.400 --> 00:25:09.937
But I'd rather not send all
that data straight back up.

00:25:09.937 --> 00:25:11.520
I'd rather just do
whatever's changed.

00:25:11.520 --> 00:25:13.140
Is there a way I can do that?

00:25:13.140 --> 00:25:14.306
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Absolutely.

00:25:14.306 --> 00:25:18.210
And the way I see it is, not
everybody knows this command

00:25:18.210 --> 00:25:19.945
because it's a Linux command.

00:25:19.945 --> 00:25:23.147
And it is actually
not that well known.

00:25:23.147 --> 00:25:23.980
MARK MANDEL: Really?

00:25:23.980 --> 00:25:25.400
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:25:25.400 --> 00:25:27.220
I mean, you have a Linux laptop.

00:25:27.220 --> 00:25:28.644
But not everybody does.

00:25:28.644 --> 00:25:29.310
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:25:29.310 --> 00:25:30.726
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, but there's

00:25:30.726 --> 00:25:32.260
this command called R-sync.

00:25:32.260 --> 00:25:34.500
Basically what it
will do is it will

00:25:34.500 --> 00:25:40.250
check the difference between
the contents of the source

00:25:40.250 --> 00:25:41.770
and the content of
the destination.

00:25:41.770 --> 00:25:44.310
And it will send
only the changes.

00:25:44.310 --> 00:25:46.967
And not only that, but it will
also the checksums to make sure

00:25:46.967 --> 00:25:48.800
that everything that
you're actually sending

00:25:48.800 --> 00:25:50.380
will be correctly received.

00:25:50.380 --> 00:25:53.920
So basically it's
a very safe way

00:25:53.920 --> 00:25:59.770
of syncing back your changes
to the original data store.

00:25:59.770 --> 00:26:02.700
Now, it is important
to be careful.

00:26:02.700 --> 00:26:06.710
Because there's one thing
which is like the dash.

00:26:06.710 --> 00:26:08.980
So dash D. In dash
D what it will do

00:26:08.980 --> 00:26:11.140
is will do it recursively.

00:26:11.140 --> 00:26:16.310
And I've done this.

00:26:16.310 --> 00:26:18.900
You know the commands have
source and destination.

00:26:18.900 --> 00:26:19.640
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:26:19.640 --> 00:26:22.430
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And source
goes first and destination

00:26:22.430 --> 00:26:23.030
goes after.

00:26:23.030 --> 00:26:24.160
MARK MANDEL: Oh, you
don't want to flip those.

00:26:24.160 --> 00:26:26.870
FRANCESC CAMPOY: If you flip
those, basically what you do--

00:26:26.870 --> 00:26:28.450
yeah, you delete all your data.

00:26:28.450 --> 00:26:30.540
So you should be
careful with that.

00:26:30.540 --> 00:26:32.390
MARK MANDEL: So dash
D does the delete?

00:26:32.390 --> 00:26:33.223
FRANCESC CAMPOY: No.

00:26:33.223 --> 00:26:35.510
Dash D, what it does
is basically recursive.

00:26:35.510 --> 00:26:36.275
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:26:36.275 --> 00:26:37.733
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So like Directory.

00:26:37.733 --> 00:26:41.200
So if you say, oh, I
want to change everything

00:26:41.200 --> 00:26:44.152
that is inside of this
place with-- like,

00:26:44.152 --> 00:26:45.860
this is the source
that's the destination

00:26:45.860 --> 00:26:47.630
and you do it backwards?

00:26:47.630 --> 00:26:48.780
Yeah, that's bad times.

00:26:48.780 --> 00:26:50.950
So you don't want to do that.

00:26:50.950 --> 00:26:54.180
And if you want to understand
better what Our Sync does.

00:26:54.180 --> 00:26:56.700
For me the way I see it
is kind of like, get.

00:26:56.700 --> 00:26:58.540
When you do get and
you don't get pull,

00:26:58.540 --> 00:27:00.870
get push, what
you're sending over

00:27:00.870 --> 00:27:03.882
the network is just the
difference between the files.

00:27:03.882 --> 00:27:05.215
That's exactly what we're doing.

00:27:05.215 --> 00:27:08.235
So if you have a huge data file.

00:27:08.235 --> 00:27:11.250
Let's say like 1
gigabyte, and you only

00:27:11.250 --> 00:27:16.600
change the first 1k of
that image-- probably it's

00:27:16.600 --> 00:27:17.660
a image or video.

00:27:17.660 --> 00:27:18.160
Whatever.

00:27:18.160 --> 00:27:18.540
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:27:18.540 --> 00:27:21.041
FRANCESC CAMPOY: You will only
send that part of the change.

00:27:21.041 --> 00:27:23.498
So it will be much, much faster
than sending the whole file

00:27:23.498 --> 00:27:24.010
back up.

00:27:24.010 --> 00:27:26.676
MARK MANDEL: So for those people
who are familiar with Our Sync,

00:27:26.676 --> 00:27:27.950
is this just using Our Sync?

00:27:27.950 --> 00:27:30.149
Or is this Our Sync
with some G Cloud tools?

00:27:30.149 --> 00:27:30.940
How does that work?

00:27:30.940 --> 00:27:32.440
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:27:32.440 --> 00:27:35.810
So Our Sync is Linux
command as we were saying.

00:27:35.810 --> 00:27:39.120
You could I guess
use Our Sync directly

00:27:39.120 --> 00:27:40.550
with Google Cloud Storage.

00:27:40.550 --> 00:27:43.060
But you're going to have to be
dealing with authentication.

00:27:43.060 --> 00:27:45.730
And I'm not really
sure it could work.

00:27:45.730 --> 00:27:48.390
So instead we have
gsutil, Our Sync,

00:27:48.390 --> 00:27:50.520
which is a
sub-command of gsutil.

00:27:50.520 --> 00:27:57.920
So you can simply say, gsutil,
Our Sync, source directory,

00:27:57.920 --> 00:28:01.590
which could be either your
local machine or somewhere

00:28:01.590 --> 00:28:03.900
in the cloud-- eventually
Google Cloud Storage.

00:28:03.900 --> 00:28:06.270
But actually you can do
it over multiple clouds.

